[1] Truckie’s stern warning to drivers after eating hot cross bun

[2] A truck driver is warning people against eating hot cross buns in the car after claims it could blow a positive on a breathalyser.

[3] A Western Australian truck driver has issued a warning for road users who like to snack on hot cross buns behind the wheel.

[4] A video posted to the Pilbara Heavy Haulage Girls Inc. Facebook page shows co-founder Heather Jones demonstrating how just one bite of the delicious snack can impact your blood alcohol level.

[5] “So, this morning we found out something interesting for all you guys and girls that are going to eat hot cross buns for breakfast or for smoko,” she says in the video.

[6] “I’m going to blow and show you something.”

[7] Ms Jones blows into the breathalyser and shows a BAC reading of 0.000.

[8] She then takes a bite out of a hot cross bun before using the breathalyser again, this time returning a reading of 0.018.

[9] “So, be really careful this Easter,” she said after the demonstration.

[10] The legal BAC limit for driving in Australia is 0.05, so if just one bite of a hot cross bun can bump the reading up to 0.018 there is the concern that a whole bun could put someone over the limit.

[11] The video has been viewed more than 3.3 million times since being uploaded on Sunday and has sparked mixed reactions from social media users.

[12] Some people claimed there are a lot of different foods that can return false readings on breathalysers, which is why users should wait 15 minutes after eating before using the device to ensure there are no traces of the food left in your mouth.

[13] One user claimed foods that have yeast in them such as hot cross buns, pizza and bread, often record BAC readings right after being eaten.

[14] “You should never do breathos straight away from eating anyway for this reason. Always wait 15 mins,” one person commented.

[15] Others found the whole situation amusing and poked fun at the discovery.

[16] “I had a 6 pack of hot cross buns last night and woke up with the biggest hangover,” one person wrote.

[17] Another claimed: “It’s a crime not heating it up and slapping butter on thing!”

[18] News.com.au has contacted the WA Police Media Unit for comment.

[19] Alcolimit, one of Australia’s leading brands of breathalysers, warns against trying to test your BAC level straight after eating or drinking.

[20] “It is imperative to not consume anything for 10-15 minutes before testing in case food or drink contains a small amount of alcohol,” the website reads.